INT. ABANDONED AIRPORT CONTROL TOWER - NIGHT

Dusty consoles. Cracked glass on all sides. A single dead radar screen. Wind whistles through broken panes.

HARLAN BEAUMONT-DIAZ (30s, flashlight, pry bar) crouches at a radio console, alone. He thumbs a dial. Static.

HARLAN
(muttering)
Dead. Of course it's dead.

The radio crackles. A burst of static. Then a voice, faint, flat.

PILOT (V.O.)
(through static)
Tower, Tower. Do you read? We are inbound. Ten miles. We have lost our lights.

Harlan freezes. The flashlight trembles.

HARLAN
This tower's been closed for thirty years.

PILOT (V.O.)
Tower, we see the runway. We are coming in. Please light the runway.

HARLAN
(into the mic)
Who is this?

PILOT (V.O.)
Tower, please. We cannot see our hands.

The radio dies. Silence. Then, from the airfield far below, a low drone. It grows. A pale glow sweeps across the cracked windows, as though something very large is descending.

Harlan backs toward the stairs, trips over the pry bar, and falls. The drone swells until the glass shivers in its frames.

HARLAN (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Not tonight. Not tonight.
